WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.777
From NVIDIA Research at CVPR 2023,

00:00:02.857 --> 00:00:07.283
this work closes a safety-critical gap in object
pose estimation.

00:00:07.363 --> 00:00:12.412
Standard two-stage pipelines detect keypoints, then
solve for the 6D pose,

00:00:12.492 --> 00:00:16.225
but give no guarantee on how wrong the answer might be.

00:00:16.305 --> 00:00:18.097
The authors add two pieces.

00:00:18.177 --> 00:00:24.474
Conformal keypoint detection turns heatmap
detections into circular or elliptical prediction

00:00:24.554 --> 00:00:29.326
sets that provably cover the true keypoints with a
chosen probability,

00:00:29.406 --> 00:00:30.643
say ninety percent.

00:00:30.723 --> 00:00:37.853
Geometric uncertainty propagation pushes those sets
through the geometry to form a Pose Uncertainty Set,

00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:40.140
or PURSE, covering the true pose.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:47.211
From PURSE they compute an average pose and use
semidefinite relaxation to bound the worst-case error.

00:00:47.291 --> 00:00:51.370
On LineMOD Occlusion, coverage is valid and the
bounds hold.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:56.455
Estimating an object's 6D pose from an image
underlies augmented reality,

00:00:56.535 --> 00:01:00.454
autonomous driving, robotic manipulation, and space
robotics.

00:01:00.534 --> 00:01:04.846
The dominant recipe is two stages: first detect
semantic keypoints,

00:01:04.926 --> 00:01:10.352
then recover the pose by minimizing reprojection
error through Perspective-n-Points.

00:01:10.432 --> 00:01:12.908
These methods score well on benchmarks,

00:01:12.988 --> 00:01:17.890
yet they return a single pose with no statement
about how trustworthy it is.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:23.200
For safety-critical systems that is a serious gap:
no guarantee on the keypoints,

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.346
no guarantee the optimizer found the right pose,

00:01:26.426 --> 00:01:30.410
and no formal bound on the worst-case error against
the truth.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:31.921
Why is this hard?

00:01:32.001 --> 00:01:34.342
The authors name three challenges.

00:01:34.422 --> 00:01:39.896
First, neural keypoint detectors can be arbitrarily
wrong, producing outliers.

00:01:39.976 --> 00:01:44.240
Second, rejecting those outliers is a nonconvex
optimization;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:51.360
fast heuristics like RANSAC are common but cannot
guarantee global optimality and can fail silently.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:57.413
Third, and most fundamental, there is no provably
correct uncertainty quantification,

00:01:57.493 --> 00:02:02.113
no formal worst-case error bound between estimate
and groundtruth.

00:02:02.193 --> 00:02:09.804
Certifying that the optimizer is globally optimal
still gives no probabilistic guarantee on the pose itself.

00:02:09.884 --> 00:02:13.720
This paper delivers exactly that guarantee, end to end.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.778
The method has two stages.

00:02:15.858 --> 00:02:17.942
In conformal keypoint detection,

00:02:18.022 --> 00:02:23.893
a calibration set of labeled images gives
nonconformity scores for the heatmap detector.

00:02:23.973 --> 00:02:26.463
Two designs are offered: a peak score,

00:02:26.543 --> 00:02:31.197
the scaled distance from the true keypoint to the
most probable pixel,

00:02:31.277 --> 00:02:35.727
and a covariance score, a Mahalanobis distance over
top detections,

00:02:35.807 --> 00:02:38.297
yielding circular and elliptical sets.

00:02:38.377 --> 00:02:45.736
Sorting the scores gives a threshold so each true
keypoint lies in its set with probability one minus epsilon.

00:02:45.816 --> 00:02:48.238
In geometric uncertainty propagation,

00:02:48.318 --> 00:02:51.754
these sets become quadratic constraints on the pose,

00:02:51.834 --> 00:02:54.054
defining the Pose Uncertainty Set.

00:02:54.134 --> 00:02:57.705
Since it is nonconvex, RANSAG samples three keypoints,

00:02:57.785 --> 00:03:02.980
solves the minimal P3P problem, keeps poses inside
the set, and averages them.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.917
Finally, maximizing the distance to the farthest
pose still inside PURSE,

00:03:07.997 --> 00:03:10.216
relaxed as a semidefinite program,

00:03:10.296 --> 00:03:13.800
certifies worst-case rotation and translation bounds.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.933
The results confirm the theory on three fronts.

00:03:18.013 --> 00:03:21.766
First, coverage is valid: at a ninety percent target,

00:03:21.846 --> 00:03:25.599
empirical coverage across objects sits right around
ninety percent and tightens as the calibration set grows.

00:03:25.679 --> 00:03:29.432
Second,

00:03:29.512 --> 00:03:33.265
the bounds are correct: in scatter plots of
certified bound versus actual error,

00:03:33.345 --> 00:03:37.098
every case where PURSE covers the true pose stays
below the diagonal,

00:03:37.178 --> 00:03:40.931
so the bound is never violated when the guarantee holds.

00:03:41.011 --> 00:03:44.764
Third, the average pose is accurate,

00:03:44.844 --> 00:03:48.597
beating prior methods on the two-dimensional
projection metric with groundtruth boxes

00:03:48.677 --> 00:03:52.430
and staying comparable with Faster-RCNN detections.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:04:00.086
The lasting message: you can turn a standard
two-stage pose estimator into one that tells you how wrong it might be,

00:04:00.166 --> 00:04:04.017
with a real statistical guarantee, and pay little
in accuracy.

00:04:04.097 --> 00:04:07.568
Conformal prediction supplies coverage on the keypoints,

00:04:07.648 --> 00:04:10.358
geometry propagates it to the pose as PURSE,

00:04:10.438 --> 00:04:15.240
and semidefinite relaxation converts the set into
concrete worst-case bounds.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:18.221
Because it wraps any heatmap keypoint detector,

00:04:18.301 --> 00:04:23.230
the approach points toward provably correct
perception wherever safety matters.
